@section Basic editing

@cindex insert mode
@cindex overwrite mode
@cindex undo
@cindex redo

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .30 .30
@headitem Action @tab Key @tab Alternative
@item Insert line break @tab @key{Return} @tab
@item Delete character left @tab @key{Backspace} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-H}
@item Delete character right @tab @key{Delete} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-D}
@item Delete next word @tab @kbd{Ctrl-T} @tab
@item Delete previous word @tab @kbd{Ctrl-O T} @tab
@item Delete to end of line @tab @kbd{Ctrl-O Y} @tab
@item Delete entire line @tab @kbd{Ctrl-Y} @tab
@item Toggle insert/overwrite @tab @key{Insert} @tab @kbd{Alt-I}
@item Indent @tab @key{Tab} @tab
@item Undo @tab @kbd{Alt-E U} @tab
@item Redo @tab @kbd{Alt-E R} @tab
@item Insert special character @tab @kbd{Alt-J} @tab
@end multitable

@kbd{Ctrl-T} at the end of a line joins the next line to it; at the end
of the text there is nothing to its right, so it deletes nothing, and
@kbd{Ctrl-Right} stays there, as in the Borland editors.  @kbd{Ctrl-O Y}
(and @kbd{Ctrl-Z}) at the end of a line deletes nothing either, and is no
undo step: the next @kbd{Ctrl-U} takes back what you did before it.

@key{Left}, @key{Right}, @key{Backspace} and @key{Delete} take one
character as the screen shows it, in a window and in a dialog field: a
whole UTF-8 character, or one byte that starts no character.

@kbd{Ctrl-U} undoes and @kbd{Ctrl-R} redoes one action at a time: an
@key{Return} or a @key{Tab} with the indent it brought, a @key{Backspace}
that re-indented a line, a deleted line, a replacement, every replacement
of one Change All, an accepted assistant Edit together with the post-edit
hook's reformatting, a block copied, moved, pasted, deleted, indented or
unindented (@kbd{Ctrl-K I}, @kbd{Ctrl-K U}), a Change Case, a character
typed over another in overwrite mode, the help file marks of
@kbd{Ctrl-O U}, @kbd{M} and @kbd{H}, the end-of-page mark of
@kbd{Ctrl-O E}.  The number of undo steps
(@samp{Options @result{} Editor}) counts these actions, never their pieces.
What was typed past the right margin moves to the next row with its word,
and @kbd{Ctrl-U} takes back exactly that there.
Undo of a deleted line puts back the line, its breakpoint, the cursor and
the block as they were before the deletion; the only line of a file is
undoable too, and so is a last line that went because its only word was
deleted.  After any Undo or Redo the block lies inside the text.  After
the Undo or Redo of an action of several pieces the block marks all the
text it brought back; when it brought back no single run of text, the
block is the one you had before the action (Undo) or the one it left
(Redo).  Breakpoints stay on their code when lines are added or
removed above them as you type (@key{Return}, a wrapped line, @kbd{Ctrl-Y},
@key{Backspace} joining two lines), by a block operation (a moved line
takes its breakpoint along) or by a tool's rewrite of the file, and
a breakpoint on a line that was deleted, alone or in a block, or joined to
the one above comes back with Undo.  The colours of a window always are those of
its text, after a tool's rewrite too.  A block move, copy, paste or delete,
an action of several pieces (@key{Return}, @key{Tab} and @key{Backspace}
with auto-indent, @kbd{Ctrl-K I} and @kbd{Ctrl-K U}, @kbd{Ctrl-O U},
@kbd{M} and @kbd{H}, Change All), and the Undo or Redo of any change, that
runs out of memory is not made at all: the text, the block, the
breakpoints and both Undo and Redo stay as they were, and the status row
says so.  An action refused because its rows would not fit a line (wider
than Max.@: Columns) is not made either, and the status row says that.
Change All that asks before each replacement stops at the one it
cannot make, as @key{Esc} would, and says so.  Change All without a prompt
needs memory for a copy of the text it searches; when there is not enough,
nothing is replaced and the status row says so.  The assistant's pane, when it cannot get a row, does the same:
it keeps what it shows and the status row says so.

@cindex read-only window
A file opened read-only (its title shows a padlock) and the clipboard
viewer (@kbd{Alt-Y}) keep their text through every command.  Typing and the
block Delete, Move and Paste do nothing there; the block commands
@kbd{Ctrl-K I}, @kbd{U}, @kbd{D} and @kbd{F}, the line commands
@kbd{Ctrl-O B}, @kbd{L}, @kbd{U}, @kbd{M}, @kbd{H} and @kbd{E}, and
Search > Replace are refused and the status row says @samp{Read-only
window: the text is unchanged}.  @key{Backspace}, with auto-indent or
without, changes nothing there and the cursor stays where it was.  Copy
still works, so their text can be taken elsewhere.

@section Block operations

@cindex blocks
@cindex selection
@cindex clipboard

Text can be selected (``marked'') for block operations.  There are two
ways to mark text:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Keyboard}: @kbd{Shift} + any cursor key extends the selection.
Or use @kbd{Ctrl-K B} to set the start and @kbd{Ctrl-K K} to set the end.

@item
@strong{Mouse}: click and drag to select.  In X11,
@kbd{Shift}-click extends the selection.
@end enumerate

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .25 .25
@headitem Action @tab Menu @tab Chord
@item Begin mark @tab @kbd{Alt-B B} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K B}
@item End mark @tab @kbd{Alt-B E} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K K}
@item Mark line @tab @kbd{Alt-B L} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K L}
@item Mark all @tab @kbd{Alt-B W} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K X}
@item Go to block start @tab @kbd{Alt-B G} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K A}
@item Go to block end @tab @kbd{Alt-B N} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K Z}
@item Copy block @tab @kbd{Alt-B C} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K C}
@item Move block @tab @kbd{Alt-B M} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K V}
@item Delete block @tab @kbd{Alt-B D} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K Y}
@item Hide marks @tab @kbd{Alt-B H} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K H}
@item Read file into block @tab @kbd{Alt-B R} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K R}
@item Write block to file @tab @kbd{Alt-B W} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K W}
@item Indent block @tab @kbd{Alt-B I} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K I}
@item Unindent block @tab @kbd{Alt-B T} @tab @kbd{Ctrl-K U}
@end multitable

Indent and unindent move each line of the block half a Tabstop.  When
the last thing an unindent takes is a tab worth more columns than that,
the columns beyond come back as blanks, so the line moves exactly half a
Tabstop.

Cut, copy, and paste use the Edit menu:

@table @kbd
@item Alt-E T
Cut (delete marked text and copy to clipboard).
@item Alt-E C
Copy (copy marked text to clipboard).
@item Alt-E P
Paste (insert clipboard at cursor).
@item Alt-E S
Show clipboard contents.
@end table

@section Search and replace

@cindex search
@cindex replace
@cindex regular expressions
@cindex regex

Open the search dialog with @kbd{Alt-S F} (Find) or @kbd{Alt-S R}
(Replace).  Both dialogs share the same options, accessible via
@kbd{Alt-@var{letter}}:

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .25 .60
@headitem Key @tab Option @tab Description
@item @kbd{Alt-T} @tab Text @tab The string to search for.
@item @kbd{Alt-N} @tab New String @tab Replacement text (Replace only).
@item @kbd{Alt-C} @tab Case Sensitive @tab Distinguish upper/lower case.
@item @kbd{Alt-W} @tab Whole Words @tab Match complete words only.
@item @kbd{Alt-R} @tab Regular Expressions @tab Enable regex mode.
@item @kbd{Alt-P} @tab Prompt @tab Ask before each replacement.
@item @kbd{Alt-D} @tab Forward @tab Search forward from cursor.
@item @kbd{Alt-B} @tab Backward @tab Search backward from cursor.
@item @kbd{Alt-G} @tab Global @tab Search the entire file.
@item @kbd{Alt-S} @tab Block @tab Search only within the marked block.
@item @kbd{Alt-F} @tab From Cursor @tab Start from current position.
@item @kbd{Alt-E} @tab From Start @tab Start from beginning of file/block.
@item @kbd{Alt-A} @tab Replace All @tab Replace all matches (Replace only).
@end multitable

Press @key{Return} to find/replace the next match, @key{ESC} to cancel.

@subsection Regular expression syntax

@cindex egrep

xwpe uses @command{egrep}-compatible regular expressions:

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@headitem Pattern @tab Meaning
@item @code{^} @tab Beginning of line.
@item @code{$} @tab End of line.
@item @code{.} @tab Any single character.
@item @code{[abc]} @tab Any character in the set.
@item @code{[^abc]} @tab Any character NOT in the set.
@item @code{[a-z]} @tab Any character in the range.
@item @code{*} @tab Previous character repeated zero or more times.
@item @code{+} @tab Previous character repeated one or more times.
@item @code{?} @tab Previous character zero or one time.
@item @code{\|} @tab Alternation (OR).
@item @code{\} @tab Escape the next character.
@end multitable

@section Markers

@cindex markers
@cindex bookmarks

xwpe supports ten position markers, numbered 0--9, as the Borland
editors have ten bookmarks.  Set a marker with @kbd{Ctrl-K @var{n}} where
@var{n} is 0--9.  Jump to a marker with @kbd{Ctrl-O @var{n}}.  Each marker
keeps its own place: setting one never moves another.  (Documented since
v1.4, as 1--9.)

@section File manager

@cindex file manager

The file manager (@kbd{Alt-F M}) provides a full-screen file browser
for opening, copying, moving, renaming, and deleting files.

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .25 .60
@headitem Key @tab Field @tab Action
@item @kbd{Alt-N} @tab Name @tab Enter a file name or mask (e.g.@: @code{*.c}).
@item @kbd{Alt-F} @tab Files @tab Select a file from the list.
@item @kbd{Alt-D} @tab Directory @tab Enter a directory path.
@item @kbd{Alt-T} @tab Tree @tab Display the directory tree.
@item @kbd{Alt-C} @tab Change @tab Change to the entered directory.
@item @kbd{Alt-E} @tab Edit @tab Open the selected file for editing.
@item @kbd{Alt-M} @tab Move @tab Rename or move a file.
@item @kbd{Alt-R} @tab Remove @tab Delete a file (to wastebasket or disk).
@item @kbd{Alt-L} @tab Link @tab Create a hard or symbolic link.
@item @kbd{Alt-O} @tab Copy @tab Copy a file.
@item @kbd{Alt-K} @tab Make Dir @tab Create a new directory.
@item @kbd{Alt-A} @tab Attributes @tab Change file permissions.
@item @kbd{Alt-S} @tab Save @tab Save with the name in the Name field.
@item @key{ESC} @tab @tab Close the file manager.
@end multitable

Additional file manager keys (documented since v1.4):

@table @kbd
@item Alt-P
Replace the current editor window with the file in the Name field.
@item Alt-U
Duplicate the marked file.  The new name is @file{@var{name}.dup}.
@end table

File masks use shell glob syntax: @code{*} matches any characters,
@code{[a-z]} matches a single character in the range.

The Name field accepts three kinds of input:

@enumerate
@item
An existing file name: pressing @key{Return} opens it for editing.
@item
A non-existing file name: pressing @key{Return} creates a new file.
@item
A file mask (e.g.@: @code{*.c}, @code{[a-z]*}): pressing @key{Return}
filters the file list.
@end enumerate

(Documented since v1.4.)

@section Automatic indentation

@cindex auto-indent
@cindex indentation

The auto-indent mode can be selected in @samp{Options @result{} Editor}
(@kbd{Alt-O E}).  Three modes are available: auto-indent for compiled
files only, auto-indent for all files, or no auto-indent.
(Documented since v1.4.)

When auto-indent is enabled, the following keys have special behaviour:

@table @key
@item Return
The new line is indented to the beginning column of the previous line.

@item Tab
If @key{Tab} is entered before the first visible character on the line,
the entire line is indented to match the previous line.  If the cursor
is past that column, the line is indented N characters beyond the
previous line (where N is the Tab Stops setting).  If the cursor is
between visible characters, a literal tab is inserted.

@item Backspace
If entered before the first visible character on a line, the line is
re-indented to match the most recent previous line that started at a
lesser column.
@end table
